{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "miriam-song",
  "text": "She watched the water close, picked up a tambourine, and started the party on the far shore.\n\nExodus fifteen. Israel has just walked through the sea on dry ground and watched it come down behind them.\n\nNow, order matters here, so let's be accurate. Moses and the children of Israel sing first. That's verse one.\n\nThen verse twenty. \"And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand. And all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances.\"\n\nAnd Miriam answered them. Answered. She takes the song and she leads the women in it, with percussion and dancing, standing on the edge of an impossible crossing.\n\n\"Sing ye to the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously. The horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.\"\n\nTwo things Scripture calls her right there in one verse. Prophetess. And sister of Aaron.\n\nExodus two never names the sister who watched a baby float away in a basket. Numbers twenty-six names Miriam among those siblings, and that's the connection most readers make.\n\nWhat isn't in doubt is this. When the water closed, she was the one who picked up an instrument.",
